# cargo-mutants writes a lock.json that records hostname+username of
# the developer who ran the sweep. That data is not load-bearing
# evidence (the cargo_mutants_version and start_time are the only
# auditable fields) and committing it leaks PII. New runs should keep
# the file out of git.
**/mutants.out/lock.json
*/lock.json

# outcomes.json carries dozens of absolute argv paths under
# `/Users/.../.rustup` that leak local workstation identity. The
# structured outcome is preserved in caught.txt / missed.txt /
# timeout.txt / unviable.txt and the per-crate `<date>.json` summary.
**/mutants.out/outcomes.json
*/outcomes.json

# cargo-mutants writes per-mutant log files (one cargo test transcript
# per mutant) under mutants.out/log/. These are useful for debugging a
# specific mutant locally but should not be committed:
# - they are very large (29MB+ per crate)
# - they have absolute paths in them (workspace-rooted)
# - the structured outcome is captured in caught.txt / missed.txt /
#   timeout.txt / unviable.txt and the per-crate <date>.json summary,
#   which IS committed.
**/mutants.out/log/

# debug.log carries the same per-mutant detail and the cargo-mutants
# version-specific timing trace. Not committed.
**/mutants.out/debug.log
